# INJECTED DEFECT -- NEVER APPLY TO A SHIPPING BUILD.
#
# Reproduces the historical dither-chasing servo, in the configuration where
# it was actually measured: raw feedback report consumed with NO averaging
# (fb_avg_mhz = mhz), at 62.5 polls/s (FB_SLOTS_HS 2, spaced 16 frames).
#
# A device expresses a rate between two representable values by dithering its
# report between them -- on this witness raw 361232 vs 361240, 8 LSB or about
# 22 ppm apart -- and the information is in the DUTY CYCLE. A rate-limited
# follower of the raw reports cannot recover that duty cycle; it walks toward
# the majority state instead. Measured at +4.8 ppm of sizing bias on this
# bench during the UAC1 feedback work, a buffer excursion every ~45 minutes.
#
# Why injected rather than historical: the EMA was never a standalone commit,
# so there is no parent to check out. Without this, W3 would ship having
# never fired on hardware.
#
# WHY THE POLL RATE IS PART OF THE DEFECT -- measured 2026-08-05. A first
# version of this patch removed the EMA alone and left the shipping 1000
# polls/s reader in place. Over 600 s it produced +0.389 ppm: seven times the
# shipping servo's -0.052 ppm, but comfortably inside the 1 ppm noise floor,
# so W3 correctly PASSed and the defect did not reproduce. The reason is that
# at one poll per frame the slew limiter (4 mHz per frame) is itself the
# low-pass filter: each raw report is superseded before the sizing rate can
# travel far toward either rail. At 62.5 polls/s a report persists for 16
# frames, the slew has time to reach a rail, and the asymmetry becomes bias.
#
# That is worth knowing on its own: the EMA's protective value shrinks as the
# poll rate approaches the device's report rate. It is not a licence to drop
# the EMA -- the horizon invariant still guards the 62.5 polls/s FS reader,
# which has no such margin.
#
# The signature to look for is R1 PASS together with W3 WARN: the host IS
# reading the feedback endpoint and still drifts. That pairing is exactly what
# W3 exists to name and what R1 alone cannot see.
#
# Apply:  git apply tools/uacvalidate/defects/w3-no-ema.patch
# Revert: git checkout -- usb_audio.cpp usb_audio.h
diff --git a/usb_audio.cpp b/usb_audio.cpp
index 79731fc..5dbbbd2 100644
--- a/usb_audio.cpp
+++ b/usb_audio.cpp
@@ -611,9 +611,8 @@ bool USBAudioOut::beginStreamingHS(const UAC1AltSetting *alt)
 				stopFeedback();
 				break;
 			}
-			// One slot per frame: each recurs every 32 ms, together
-			// 1000 polls/s -- every report the device publishes.
-			uint16_t frame = (uint16_t)k;
+			/* INJECTED: 16 frames apart, the historical spacing. */
+			uint16_t frame = (uint16_t)(k * 16);
 			itd_link(periodic_frame_slot(frame), fb_itd[k], frame);
 		}
 	}
@@ -756,8 +755,9 @@ void USBAudioOut::service()
 					// as FS (raw-chasing measured +4.8 ppm on
 					// this bench). The divisor holds the 128 ms
 					// horizon at this reader's 250 polls/s.
-					fb_avg_mhz = uac1_fb_average(fb_avg_mhz, mhz,
-					                             FB_EMA_DIV_HS);
+					/* INJECTED DEFECT: chase the raw report,
+					 * no averaging at all. */
+					fb_avg_mhz = mhz;
 					fb_frames_since = 0;
 					fb_packets++;
 				} else {
